@startuml
title UART RTS/CTS — the handshake as firmware, and the invariant it guarantees
' Sources: firmware/uart_flow.pe (the poll, the dispatch, the bit loop),
'          tb/tb_pe_soc_uart_flow.v (the invariant monitor, the 8N1 receiver,
'          the two cases), tools/fw/peasm.py (UTX/URX/RTS/CTS, TIMER)
' Pin map, the shared 8-bit port: bit 0 TX (out)  bit 3 RX (in)
'                                 bit 4 RTS (out) bit 5 CTS (in)
' Program: payload 0x00 0xFF 0xA5, one RTS frame around each byte
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam state {
  FontSize 11
  BackgroundColor<<tx>>   #dce9f8
  BorderColor<<tx>>       #4a6fa5
  FontColor<<tx>>         #16324f
  BackgroundColor<<hs>>   #fbe9cf
  BorderColor<<hs>>       #b8813a
  FontColor<<hs>>         #5a3d12
  BackgroundColor<<wait>> #dceee0
  BorderColor<<wait>>     #4a8a5c
  FontColor<<wait>>       #17452a
  BackgroundColor<<box>>  #f2f2f2
  BorderColor<<box>>      #808080
  FontColor<<box>>        #303030
}

[*] --> init
state "init\nLDI A, UTX / OUT TXPIN\nTX idles HIGH, RTS RELEASED\n(bits 4 and 8..: 8 payloads live\nin dmem[8], dmem[9], dmem[10])" as init
state "main\nLDM A,2 (bytes sent)\nSUB A,3 / JZ done" as main <<box>>
state "ASSERT RTS FIRST\nLDI A, RTS|UTX / OUT TXPIN\nthe line is still idle, so nothing\nhas been transmitted yet\ndmem[4] = 0x10" as rts <<hs>>
state "wait_cts — A POLL, NOT A TIMEOUT\nIN A,PIN / AND A,CTS / JNZ cts_go\nelse dmem[3]++ and saturate at 0xFF:\na real driver bounds this wait and gives\nup on a dead receiver; there is no\nretry policy here" as wait <<wait>>
state "fetch the payload by DISPATCH\nLDS A,[X] is X-indexed with NO\noffset, so dmem[8+index] is not\naddressable: three compares and\nthree loads, not a table lookup" as fetch <<box>>
state "tx_start — 2 TIMER ticks\nRTS is re-asserted in the value:\nLDI A, RTS  (the start bit is TX low\nwith RTS still high)" as txs <<tx>>
state "tx_loop — 8 data bits, LSB first\nAND A,1 / OR A,RTS / OUT TXPIN\nRTS stays high for the WHOLE frame:\ndropping it here would tell the\nreceiver the line is free mid-byte" as txl <<tx>>
state "tx_stop — 2 more ticks\nLDI A, RTS|UTX\nTX high for the stop bit,\nRTS still asserted" as txp <<tx>>
state "RELEASE RTS, AND ONLY NOW\nthe last stop bit has been clocked\nout, so the line is ours no longer\nOUT TXPIN with UTX, dmem[4] = 0x00" as rel <<hs>>
state "count\ndmem[1]++ index, dmem[2]++ sent" as cnt <<box>>
state "done\ndmem[12] = 0xA5" as done
state "park" as park

init --> main
main --> rts : sent < 3
rts --> wait
wait --> fetch : CTS is HIGH
wait --> wait : CTS is LOW, dmem[3]++
fetch --> txs
txs --> txl
txl --> txl : dmem[6]--, 8 bits
txl --> txp
txp --> rel
rel --> cnt
cnt --> main
main --> done : sent == 3
done --> park
park --> park : JMP park

note right of wait
  WHY ASSERT BEFORE WAIT. The receiver
  raises CTS IN RESPONSE to RTS. A driver
  that waited for CTS before asserting RTS
  would wait for ever on a receiver that
  only raises CTS when asked — a deadlock
  no amount of correct bit-banging fixes,
  and one a testbench that drives CTS
  low-then-high can make look like a hang
  rather than like an ordering mistake.
end note
note right of wait
  THE SATURATION IS NOT COSMETIC. The loop
  runs one iteration every six clocks, so
  a receiver holding CTS low for a
  millisecond wraps an 8-bit counter
  several times over. Saturating at 0xFF
  makes dmem[3] a statement about "it
  waited, and at least this long" instead
  of a residue — which is what the TB's
  second case requires.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
title UART RTS/CTS — one byte is ten cells, and the ninth clock is the handshake
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

actor "receiver" as R #dceee0
participant "RTS\nbit 4" as RTS #fbe9cf
participant "CTS\nbit 5" as CTS #fbe9cf
participant "TX\nbit 0" as TX
actor "master" as M

R -> RTS : "I am ready to receive"
M -> RTS : RTS HIGH — asserted BEFORE CTS is read
R -> CTS : CTS HIGH
note over RTS, CTS
  ORDER IS ASSERTED, NOT INFERRED. A driver
  that waited for CTS first deadlocks against
  a receiver that only raises CTS in response
  to RTS, and the TB's second case is exactly
  what such a receiver looks like.
end note
participant "start" as st
participant "d0" as d0
participant "d1" as d1
participant "d2" as d2
participant "d3" as d3
participant "d4" as d4
participant "d5" as d5
participant "d6" as d6
participant "d7" as d7
participant "stop" as sp
participant "next" as nx

M -> st : TX low
st -> d0 : one bit = 2 TIMER ticks = 520 clks = 8.667 us
d0 -> d1
d1 -> d2
d2 -> d3
d3 -> d4
d4 -> d5
d5 -> d6
d6 -> d7
d7 -> sp
sp -> nx : RTS released here, and only here
note over d0, d7
  LSB FIRST, and the payload is 0x00, 0xFF, 0xA5
  on purpose. 0x00 is eight zero bits behind a
  start bit and 0xFF is eight one bits, so a bit
  period a little long or a little short shows up
  in one of them and not the other; and 0xA5
  (1010 0101) is not a bit palindrome, so a
  transmitter that shifted the wrong way is
  visible rather than accidentally correct.
end note
note over sp
  10 cells per byte, 2 ticks each.
  RTS is re-asserted inside every single
  OUT, so it cannot be dropped by a
  refactor of the bit loop.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
title UART RTS/CTS — case 1: CTS held low for 400 us, then released
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

actor "TB receiver\ndrives CTS" as R #dceee0
participant "CTS" as C #fbe9cf
participant "RTS" as S #fbe9cf
participant "TX" as T
actor "master\npoll loop" as M

R -> C : CTS LOW for 24,000 clocks
activate M
M -> C : read CTS, find it LOW
loop the wait — one iteration every six clocks, dmem[3] saturates at 0xFF
  M -> C : read CTS again
  C --> M : still LOW
end
R -> C : CTS released
C --> M : HIGH — the wait exits
M -> S : first start bit, at or after the CTS rise
M -> T : 0x00, then 0xFF, then 0xA5
M -> S : RTS released after the last stop bit
deactivate M
note over C, T
  THE INVARIANT, CHECKED EVERY CLOCK:
  TX NEVER LEAVES IDLE WHILE CTS IS LOW.
  Not "the program eventually transmits" —
  a bare echo satisfies that. Not even "it
  waits for CTS before its first start bit",
  which a one-shot test satisfies by
  accident if the wait sits after the frame.
  The claim is about EVERY instant CTS is
  low, and it is the only one of the three a
  receiver with a full buffer can rely on.
end note
note over S
  RTS MUST BE HIGH FOR EVERY INSTANT TX
  IS NOT IDLE. TX low means this program is
  mid-frame, and RTS low at that instant
  tells the receiver the line is free while
  we are driving it — the failure a
  "release RTS when the buffer is empty"
  implementation makes, and invisible to a
  check that only compares the first and
  last edge of the handshake.
end note
note over M
  Measured: CTS released at 417.225 us;
  the three bytes decode at 499.874,
  585.853 and 672.517 us, i.e. a frame
  period of 85.98 and 86.66 us for ten
  cells. dmem[3] = 255 (saturated), which
  is the non-vacuity half: a program that
  never consulted CTS would leave it 0
  and pass every other check here.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
!pragma teoz
title UART RTS/CTS — the 400 us wait, to scale; one axis unit is 10 us
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
concise "CTS" as C
concise "RTS" as S
concise "TX" as T
concise "wire" as W
concise "nums" as N
@0
C is 0
S is 0
T is 1
W is "idle"
N is "TX idle HIGH"
@1
S is 1
N is "RTS asserted FIRST"
@42
N is "the wait spins"
@43
C is 1
N is "CTS rises 417.2 us"
@44
W is "byte 0 = 0x00"
N is "first start bit"
@52
W is "byte 1 = 0xFF"
N is "RTS still high"
@61
W is "byte 2 = 0xA5"
N is "86.66 us per frame"
@65
S is 0
N is "RTS released"
@70
W is "idle"
N is "3 bytes decoded"
@80
N is "case 2: no wait ends"
@90
N is "the wire never moved"
@100
N is "dmem[3] = 255, dmem[2] = 0"
@endsync
@enduml

